# swmm5-cplus: EPA SWMM 5.2.4 as reentrant C++17, byte-identical to EPA

## What it does

swmm5-cplus is EPA SWMM 5.2.4 restructured so that each simulation is a C++ object (`Project`) instead of
process-wide globals. A program can open, step and couple any number of models at once. Every computed
number is EPA's: `.out` byte for byte, `.rpt` byte for byte after removing the three wall-clock lines.
The app runs EPA 5.2.4 and swmm5-cplus side by side in the browser (both compiled to WebAssembly), compares
their outputs value by value, runs six models in one engine instance, and runs a two-model coupling demo.
Its Doc tab is the essay *Same Numbers, Different Bones*, which also compares SWMM6 (OpenSWMM).

## How it computes

- Engine: EPA SWMM 5.2.4 source, lifted by `tools/make_cplus.py` (libclang). {MUTABLE} mutable file-scope
  variables became `Project` fields; {FUNCS} functions became members; {EDITS} edits, each at a location
  the compiler reported. No arithmetic was changed.
- Six gated steps: Stage 1 (lift), R1 (resource safety), R2 (root finder, ODE solver, expression parser as
  standalone components), R3 (cross sections as shape classes, `tools/transpose_xsect.py`), R3b (shape
  table at namespace scope, C++ facade, coupling example), R4 (outfall normal and critical depth reused
  when the flow is bitwise equal to the last call's; profile-guided build).
- APIs: EPA's {EPA_API} `swmm_` functions unchanged (one default project per thread), plus
  {CP_HANDLE} `swmm5cp_` handle functions and a header-only `swmm::Simulation` class.
- Browser: Clang 18 + wasi-libc, reactor modules; files live in an in-memory WASI file system (13 calls).
- Coupling demo: two models stepped in lock step; flow at upstream outfall OA is added as lateral inflow
  at downstream node IN after each routing step (one-way).

## Inputs and outputs

- Reads any SWMM 5.2.4 `.inp` plus the data files it names (rain, inflow, hot-start); nothing is added to
  or stripped from the `.inp`.
- Writes `.rpt` and `.out` in EPA's formats; comparison tables export as CSV; the essay downloads as `.docx`.

## Checks

Mechanical checks, from the repository's result files:

| Check | Result |
|---|---|
| Full corpus gate, native, swmm5-cplus vs EPA 5.2.4 | {FULL_SAME} of {FULL_DONE} identical; {FULL_CRASH} crash decks crash both alike; {FULL_DIFF} different; {FULL_INC} inconclusive |
| WebAssembly gate, smoke + coverage sets | {WASM_SAME} of {WASM_N} identical |
| Six models in one instance, round-robin | each `.out` identical to its solo EPA run |
| Speed, exact builds, 8 models, CPU time | R4 with PGO: {SP_R4} of EPA 5.2.4's time; noise measured by timing EPA against itself |
| Four-engine gate, SWMM6 {S6_COMMIT_TXT}, {S6_ALL} models | swmm5-cplus vs EPA identical on {S6_EC} of {S6_EC_N}; SWMM6 legacy vs EPA {S6_EL} of {S6_EL_N}; SWMM6 new vs legacy {S6_LN} of {S6_LN_N}; SWMM6 new vs EPA {S6_EN} of {S6_EN_N} |
| SWMM6 controls (each engine run twice) | legacy {S6_LL} of {S6_LL_N} identical; new {S6_NN} of {S6_NN_N} identical |
| FP contraction, SWMM6 c40580c built with and without | new-engine output changed on {UP_NB_CHANGED} models ({UP_NB_BIG} by over 10 % of an element's peak); new vs legacy identical {UP_A_LN} -> {UP_B_LN}; legacy unchanged |
| Code, SWMM6 c40580c -> db4634a, both without contraction | legacy changed on {UP_CODE_L} models, new on {UP_CODE_N}; against EPA 5.2.4: {UP_FURTHER} further, {UP_CLOSER} closer (runs that hit the time limit left out) |
| Bisection, {UP_SRC_COMMITS} commits touching source, {UP_BISECT_N} models, {UP_BISECT_BUILT} builds | `40f439a` (conduit evaporation and seepage limited by node water) changed {UP_C1_N}; all have evaporation or seepage, none of {UP_C1_NOLOSS} dynamic-wave models without either changed; six more commits changed 1 to 6 models each |
| CPU time vs EPA 5.2.4, SWMM6 `db4634a` | new engine {S6_SP_SWMM6_NEW} on 8 small models (contraction on {S6_SP_SWMM6_NEW_A}, off {S6_SP_SWMM6_NEW_B}), {S6_LG_BIG} on 8,683 links; legacy {S6_SP_SWMM6_LEGACY}; noise {S6_SP_NOISE} |

Difference classes: largest change in any link flow or node depth over the run, as a percent of that
element's peak, above a floor of 1 L/s and 1 mm. Periods are matched by time stamp.

Human review: none of the generated C++ or hand edits has been reviewed line by line by a person.

## What this does not show

1. Not more accurate. Every number is EPA 5.2.4's, its numerical defects included; no field data were used.
2. Native and browser runs are not promised identical bits; EPA's own C differs native vs WebAssembly on {PLAT_DIFF} of {WASM_N} models.
3. Fixes none of EPA's crashes; the {FULL_CRASH} crash decks crash both engines alike.
4. Thread safety was tested (six models, six threads), not audited.
5. The coupling is a one-way demonstration, not a validated method.
6. SWMM6 is alpha. Its gate is SWMM5 models, which exercise its legacy-compatible paths, not its new physics; it was built without GeoPackage, HDF5, 2D and GPU. A difference from 5.2.4 is a difference, not a defect.
7. All timing on one two-core machine; nothing over 8,683 links was timed.
8. The first SWMM6 build (FP contraction on) was reported in the first version of the essay as if it were the repository's build. It was not; this version corrects it and reports both.

Defect register (swmm5-cplus findings in EPA 5.2.4): S5CP-1 to S5CP-10, in the essay's "What turned up along the way".

## How to reproduce

- swmm5-cplus gate: `tools/gate.py` on any folder of models (EPA built from tag v5.2.4 at -O2).
- SWMM6 gate: `tools/swmm6/cmp6u.py` (three builds), then `views6u.py`, `summarize.py`, `moves6u.py`,
  `lngroups6u.py`, `flagfeat6u.py`; commits: `tools/swmm6/bisect6.py`. Results in `results/swmm6/db4634a/`.
- Timing: `tools/perf/bench_variants.py`, interleaved, three repetitions, CPU time.
- Corpus: SWMMBobSWMM6/1729-SWMM5-Models-2030.

